  </p>

  <h2 id="second-stage">The second seat after the BSc is a separate race</h2>

  <p>
    For the pure-science BSc courses, the first competition is the college seat. The second is the MSc, NET or research seat. A path can clear the first and still stall at the second.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Biotechnology, microbiology, zoology and botany graduates usually reach an MSc through IIT JAM or CUET PG. JAM has a single Biotechnology paper for the whole life-science group, and in 2026 about one registrant in five qualified in it (<a href="/blog/career-options/bsc-career-options/">worked out in the BSc career options guide</a>).</li>
    <li>A fellowship or lecturer path adds a third filter: NET, a funded PhD seat, then a scarce permanent post.</li>
    <li>Nursing, lab technology and dietetics have a lighter second stage. Their ladder is a specialisation or a practice, not a rank.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The routes after the degree, subject by subject, are in <a href="/blog/career-options/career-after-bsc-for-pcb-students/">career after BSc for PCB students</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="multiplier">The multiplier skill, the proof and the AI effect for each BSc</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>A high-value skill portfolio beats a degree title.</strong> One strong core skill, one multiplier skill and visible proof is the stack that lifts pay in any of these routes, and it is what moves you toward higher income options sooner. AI changes which tasks stay yours.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>BSc lane</th><th>Multiplier skill</th><th>Proof to build</th><th>AI leverage</th><th>AI risk</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {multiplierRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="BSc lane"><strong>{row.lane}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Multiplier skill">{row.skill}</td>
            <td data-label="Proof to build">{row.proof}</td>
            <td data-label="AI leverage">{row.ai}</td>
            <td data-label="AI risk">{row.risk}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Start by using AI to summarise papers and draft handouts, then check every claim yourself. Later, build a workflow in your own field, such as a cleaned dataset, a quality checklist or a patient-education pack, and keep it as proof. Tool use alone does not raise pay, and no multiplier is guaranteed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Clear English, spoken and written, lifts every lane here, because you will explain results to patients, clients and managers. Talk to two or three people who do the job before you pay a fee; one real conversation beats twenty opinions from relatives. The data lane can also be done remotely, while nursing, lab and field work cannot, so pick the work style you can live with.
  </p>

  <p>
    Free learning is enough to test a skill. Sample a strong YouTube series or a reputable low-cost course on statistics, Python or lab quality first, and judge each course by its teacher, update date and the work it makes you produce. Pay only for feedback, structure or a credential an employer values. A pile of certificates is not a portfolio.
  </p>

  <h2 id="business">The business layer, and how clients find you</h2>

  <p>
    Some of these routes are employer-only. Government posts and hospital nursing jobs pay a salary, so sales skill matters mainly for a side route or a later move. The routes below can become a practice or a service.
  </p>

